@node	0.6, 0.7, 0.5, Chapter 0
@section Closures and trait objects

A @newterm{closure} is an anonymous function that can capture bindings from
the scope around it, Rust's equivalent of the source book's @code{lambda}.  Closures
are first-class from section 1.3 on, the same way procedures are in the book.

@example rust
fn make_multiplier(factor: i64) -> impl Fn(i64) -> i64 @{
    move |x| x * factor
@}

let triple = make_multiplier(3);
let result = triple(7);
println!("@{result@}");
// => 21
@end example

@code{make_multiplier} returns a closure, so it needs to say what kind of
callable it returns; @code{impl Fn(i64) -> i64} means ``some concrete type
that can be called like this,'' without naming the closure's actual
(compiler-generated, unnameable) type. The @code{move} keyword forces the
closure to capture @code{factor} by value rather than by reference, which
matters once the closure needs to outlive the function that created it, as
this one does.

Three traits distinguish closures by what they do with their captures.
@code{Fn} borrows its captures and can be called any number of times;
@code{FnMut} borrows them mutably and can also be called repeatedly, but only
through a mutable reference to the closure itself; @code{FnOnce} may consume
its captures and so can be called at most once. Every closure implements at
least @code{FnOnce}; a closure that only reads its captures implements
@code{Fn} too, and one that mutates a captured binding without consuming it
implements @code{FnMut} but not @code{Fn}.

@example rust
fn call_three_times(mut f: impl FnMut() -> i32) -> Vec<i32> @{
    vec![f(), f(), f()]
@}

let mut count = 0;
let results = call_three_times(|| @{
    count += 1;
    count
@});
println!("@{results:?@}");
// => [1, 2, 3]
@end example

The closure above mutates @code{count} on every call, so it needs
@code{FnMut}; @code{call_three_times} takes its parameter as @code{mut f} so
it can call @code{f} through a mutable reference to itself, which is exactly
what @code{FnMut} requires. Passing this same closure where a plain
@code{Fn} was expected would not compile: mutating a capture is precisely
what @code{Fn} forbids.

@code{impl Fn(i64) -> i64} and @code{impl FnMut() -> i32} work as return or
parameter types only because every path through the function agrees on one
concrete (if unnameable) closure type. A function that might hand back one of
several different closures depending on a runtime condition needs a
different mechanism, since @code{impl Trait} cannot describe ``one of these
two different types'':

@example rust
fn apply_all(ops: &[Box<dyn Fn(i64) -> i64>], x: i64) -> Vec<i64> @{
    ops.iter().map(|op| op(x)).collect()
@}

let ops: Vec<Box<dyn Fn(i64) -> i64>> =
    vec![Box::new(make_multiplier(2)), Box::new(|x| x + 100)];
let results = apply_all(&ops, 5);
println!("@{results:?@}");
// => [10, 105]
@end example

@code{dyn Fn(i64) -> i64} is a @newterm{trait object}: a value known only by
which trait it implements, stored behind a pointer (@code{Box} here) because
its concrete size is not known at compile time. A @code{Vec} or slice of
@code{Box<dyn Fn(...)>} can hold closures of different concrete types side by
side, the way this book's dispatch tables (@ref{2.4}) hold procedures of
different shapes under one type, and its evaluators (@ref{4.1}) represent
every primitive and compound procedure as one @code{Value} variant wrapping
exactly this kind of boxed closure.

The runnable listings above are @code{examples/sec_0_6_closures.rs} and
@code{examples/sec_0_6_fn_mut.rs}.
